+# A browser UI for the whole program
+
+`python ui.py` serves a local web app on 127.0.0.1:8765: postings with a
+reading pane and application status, draggable tier thresholds, scans with
+live logs and a digest preview, drafts (view, edit, create, bulk), scoring
+tools, model assignment per role and track, config and resume editors, and
+preflight. It runs the existing scripts rather than re-implementing them.
+
+New files: `ui.py`, `ui/index.html`. `score.py --backfill --uid UID` scores
+a single posting (the UI's Rescore button). The `status` column in
+`seen.db`, created long ago and never used, now holds new / saved / applied
+/ dismissed. Config saves from the UI keep comments and write a backup to
+`backups/`.
+
+---
+
+# Every AI call runs on a model you choose
+
+All three AI jobs - the jev scorer, the rubric scorer (the original hosted
+one) and tailor.py - now go through `llm.py` and run on any model: Anthropic,
+OpenAI, Ollama, LM Studio, llama.cpp, vLLM, OpenRouter, Groq, Together,
+DeepSeek, Mistral, Gemini, xAI, any OpenAI-compatible server, JevK5, or
+LiteLLM. No vendor SDK is required; `anthropic` left requirements.txt.
+
+**config.yaml.** New `models:` (named profiles) and `ai:` (which profile does
+which job, optionally per track). The llama.cpp settings moved out of `jev:`
+into the `jevk5` profile. `scoring.provider` is now `scoring.method` (jev /
+rubric / keyword); the old key and "anthropic" are still read. A config with
+neither new block behaves exactly as before.
+
+**jev on other models.** Questions keep JevK5's prompt; answers are read from
+logprobs (llama.cpp, vLLM, OpenAI non-reasoning models, Ollama via its native
+API), from probabilities the model writes out (`verbalized`, works anywhere),
+or by voting. `auto` tries logprobs and falls back once, loudly. Hosted
+backends can run postings in parallel (`concurrency`).
+
+**Cost cap follows the model.** `max_per_run` counts postings sent to
+`cost: paid` profiles, whichever method. Only the local presets default to
+free; a localhost proxy does not, since it may bill a hosted API. A free jev
+run that fails and falls back to a paid rubric is capped too (previously it
+was not).
+
+**Scores remember their model.** New `scorer_model` column (added on open).
+`scorer` now stores `rubric` instead of `anthropic`; old rows still read as
+rubric everywhere. `--calibrate` warns per model, `--backfill --scorer`
+accepts a profile name.
+
+**Tools.** `score.py --compare --left SPEC --right SPEC` compares any two
+method:model pairs. `preflight.py --probe` calls each model once and reports
+the readout that worked. `tailor.py --model`, `bench_jev.py --model`,
+`diag_career.py --model`, and `JOBSCAN_AI="role=profile"` for one-off runs.
+
+**jev's default is your local JevK5.** With `ai.jev` unset, no `ai:` block,
+an unassigned track, or jev.py run directly, jev uses the `jevk5` profile
+(your llama-server and GGUF, calibration 1.22 / 0.93), started on demand with
+the same command as before. It never defaults to a hosted model.
+
+**Kept from the Anthropic SDK** after it was removed: the 600s request
+timeout, `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` and `ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN`, retries on
+408/409/429/5xx/529, and per-batch recovery from a hosted network blip. A
+refused connection to a LOCAL server still stops that model for the run.
+
+**Behaviour changes.** A free jev run that fails over to a paid rubric model
+is now held to `max_per_run`; it used to bypass the cap entirely. Connection
+failures to a local server end that model's batches at once instead of
+failing each one. `--calibrate`'s mixed-scorer warning no longer counts
+keyword/rule/over-cap rows as a scorer. And `--no-score` / `scoring.enabled: false` now skips jev
+too. It used to run jev anyway, despite the help text saying "skip the model".
+
+---
+
