+## The browser UI
+
+```bash
+python ui.py            # opens http://127.0.0.1:8765
+```
+
+Everything the scripts do, in one place, through the same code:
+
+- **Postings.** Everything in `seen.db`, by track, tier, scorer, date or
+  search, with the full posting in a reading pane. Mark each one saved,
+  applied or dismissed; `j`/`k` move, `o` opens the posting, `s`/`a`/`d`/`n`
+  set the status. The strip at the top shows every score from 0 to 100: drag
+  the maybe and strong lines to see how the tiers would change, then save
+  them to `config.yaml`.
+- **Run a scan.** `main.py` with every flag, its log streamed live, and a
+  preview of the digest email.
+- **Drafts.** Analyses, letters and resume advice from `out/`, editable.
+  Draft from any posting, a pasted one, or in bulk.
+- **Scoring.** Test a title, backfill, rescore, compare two scorers,
+  calibrate, benchmark jev.
+- **Models.** Which model does each job, per track. Probe them all.
+- **Settings / Resume facts.** Edit `config.yaml` and `resume.yaml` with
+  validation. Every save copies the old file into `backups/` first, and
+  structured changes (thresholds, models) rewrite only the line they touch,
+  so your comments survive.
+- **Health.** Preflight, `.env` status, job-board list refresh.
+
+It listens on 127.0.0.1 only and runs nothing but the jobscan scripts, with
+flags from a fixed list. Standard library only; nothing new to install.
+Pasted postings are kept in `out/_ui/`.
+
+## Choosing models
+
+Every AI call runs on a model you pick. Three jobs use one:
+
+| Role | What it does | Needs |
+|---|---|---|
+| `jev` | band and yes/no questions per posting (score.py, `method: jev`) | probabilities: JevK5, a model with logprobs, or any model in `verbalized` mode |
+| `rubric` | the prose rubric, ~20 postings per call (`method: rubric`, and jev's fallback) | any generative model with a decent context |
+| `tailor` | viability analysis, letters, resume advice | the strongest model you will pay for; split into `tailor_analyze` / `tailor_letter` / `tailor_resume` if you like |
+
+Define profiles under `models:` in `config.yaml`, then assign them under `ai:`:
+
+```yaml
+models:
+  qwen: {provider: ollama, model: "qwen3:8b", extra_body: {reasoning_effort: none}, batch_size: 4}
+  mini: {provider: openai, model: "gpt-4.1-mini", concurrency: 6}
+ai:
+  jev: {career: qwen, bridge: jevk5}
+  rubric: mini
+  tailor: sonnet
+```
+
+Providers: `anthropic`, `openai`, `ollama`, `lmstudio`, `llamacpp`, `vllm`,
+`openrouter`, `groq`, `together`, `deepseek`, `mistral`, `gemini`, `xai`,
+`openai_compatible` (any `/v1/chat/completions` server), `jevk5`, and
+`litellm` (Bedrock, Vertex, Azure and the rest; `pip install litellm`). Keys
+come from each preset's environment variable (`OPENAI_API_KEY`,
+`OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, ...) or `api_key_env:`.
+
+One run on a different model, no edits:
+
+```bash
+JOBSCAN_AI="rubric=qwen,jev.bridge=mini" python main.py
+python tailor.py 787428 --model qwen
+python bench_jev.py --model qwen
+```
+
+Before trusting a new model:
+
+```bash
+python preflight.py --probe                         # one tiny call to each model in use
+python score.py --compare --right jev:qwen          # vs the rubric scores already stored
+python score.py --compare --left rubric:haiku --right rubric:qwen --track career
+```
+
+The `jev:` thresholds were tuned on JevK5. Another model's probabilities sit
+on a different scale; `calibration_temperature` on its profile and the
+`--compare` output are how you line them up. `--calibrate` reports each model
+separately, because a Haiku 72 and a Qwen 72 are not the same number.
+
+Cost: `scoring.max_per_run` caps postings sent to `cost: paid` models.
+Ollama, LM Studio, llama.cpp, vLLM and JevK5 default to free; everything else
+is paid unless you say otherwise. jev on a hosted model is four calls per
+posting, so set `concurrency` and expect it to cost more than the rubric.
+
